//! stdlib/algebra/sedenion_verdict.sio
//!
//! Phase 3 — the TYPED EPISTEMIC BOUNDARY between an exact proof and a float measurement.
//!
//! A zero-divisor verdict carries its PROVENANCE as a first-class value. The type system
//! makes a proof (`Proved`) and a measurement (`MeasuredF64`) DISTINCT enum variants, so a
//! consumer that demands a proof cannot silently accept a tolerance-gated float result.
//!
//!     Invariant: exactness is a property of the CONTRACT, not of the number.
//!
//! Compiler-capability note (souc v0.80.0): the operator's Q-B choice was to wrap the verdict
//! in `Knowledge<Verdict>`. A function RETURNING `Knowledge<Verdict>` hits the generic-struct-
//! return gap (#1, fable5 fix in flight), so this ships-now layer returns the plain `Verdict`
//! enum. The enum is PAYLOAD-FREE — the current souc rejects data-carrying variants (E200, D7);
//! see the NOTE below. Knowledge-wrapping is a drop-in once #1 lands.

/// Provenance of a zero-divisor annihilation verdict.
///   Proved         — a·b == 0 by exact integer (ℤ) vector equality. Decidable. No tolerance.
///   MeasuredF64    — ||a·b||² < eps under f64. A measurement, NOT a proof.
///   MeasuredF256   — future high-precision witness shape only (no f256 arithmetic here).
// NOTE: PAYLOAD-FREE. The intended type carries the tolerance (`MeasuredF64 { eps: f64 }`,
// `MeasuredF256 { eps256: f64 }`), but the current souc rejects data-carrying enum variants (E200
// "undefined variable" on the field name; see docs/handoff/souc_v0800_defects.md D7). The
// boundary guarantee (proof vs measurement) is carried by the variant tag alone; the eps tolerance
// travels alongside in practice and the payload returns once the compiler supports it.
pub enum Verdict {
    Proved,
    MeasuredF64,
    MeasuredF256,
}

// ── Explicit constructors: the ONLY ways to mint each provenance. No implicit coercion. ──

/// The exact proof tag. Construct ONLY when exact annihilation has been established.
pub fn verdict_proved() -> Verdict { Verdict::Proved }

/// The f64 measurement tag, carrying the tolerance it was gated on.
pub fn verdict_measured_f64() -> Verdict { Verdict::MeasuredF64 }

/// The f256 measurement tag (boundary shape only — f256 arithmetic is intentionally unimplemented).
pub fn verdict_measured_f256() -> Verdict { Verdict::MeasuredF256 }

// ── Gate composition ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The exact gate `zd_exact : a·b == 0 in ℤ → Proved` is the decidable detector
// `zd_exact_i64(a, b)` (in cayley_dickson_exact_i64.sio) composed with `verdict_proved()`:
//
//     if zd_exact_i64(a, b) { verdict_proved() }        // exact annihilation → Proved
//
// The f64 gate `zd_float64 : ||a·b||² < eps → MeasuredF64` composes the (out-of-this-build,
// see EXACT_CORE.md) tolerance detector with `verdict_measured_f64(eps)`. Crucially, the two
// gates mint DIFFERENT variants, and there is no function that turns one into the other — so a
// measurement can never be laundered into a proof. See the run-pass demo
// tests/run-pass/sedenion_verdict_boundary.sio.

// ── Consumer that ENFORCES the boundary: accepts a proof, rejects a measurement ──────────────

/// A sink that requires an exact PROOF. Returns true ONLY for `Proved`; a `MeasuredF64` or
/// `MeasuredF256` is rejected. This is where the type system blocks proof↔measurement mixing —
/// the single load-bearing guarantee of the boundary. "Is this a measurement?" is exactly its
/// negation: `!requires_proof(v)`.
///
/// NB (souc v0.80.0): a redundant sibling `is_measurement` with the mirror-image match returned a
/// WRONG result for `MeasuredF64` while this `requires_proof` gives the full correct truth table —
/// a data-carrying-enum `match` codegen flake when two matches over one enum sit in one module.
/// We therefore expose only `requires_proof` and derive the complement by negation.
pub fn requires_proof(v: Verdict) -> bool {
    match v {
        Verdict::Proved => true,
        Verdict::MeasuredF64 => false,
        Verdict::MeasuredF256 => false,
    }
}
